Relayium — License Index
Copyright (c) 2026 Relayium

Relayium is open core: different directories in this repository are
licensed differently, according to the trust boundary each one sits on.
There is no single license that covers the whole repository — consult
the table below and the `LICENSE` file inside the relevant directory.

| Directory            | License                                   | Full text                          |
|-----------------------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| `server/`             | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 (AGPL-3.0-only) | [`server/LICENSE`](server/LICENSE) |
| `web/`                | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 (AGPL-3.0-only) | [`web/LICENSE`](web/LICENSE)       |
| `apps/`               | Apache License 2.0                        | [`apps/LICENSE`](apps/LICENSE)     |
| `docs/protocol/`      | Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0) | [`docs/protocol/LICENSE`](docs/protocol/LICENSE) |

Everything else in the repository (top-level docs, build config, CI, etc.)
is not itself a distributable component and is not separately licensed;
treat it as covered by the license of the directory it configures or
documents unless stated otherwise.

## Why three licenses

- **AGPL-3.0** on `server/` and `web/`: this is the signaling server, relay
  node, CLI, and the Svelte web client — the parts that make up a running
  Relayium *service*. AGPL's network-use clause means that anyone who
  modifies this code and offers it as a hosted service to others must
  publish their modified source. That is what stops a competitor from
  taking the whole stack — including the SEO article content under
  `web/scripts/pages/` — and standing up a rival hosted service without
  giving anything back. It is still OSI-approved open source: self-hosters
  keep every right they had before (run it, modify it, redistribute it),
  they just can't turn a private fork into an unshared competing service.
  Grafana, MinIO, and Mastodon all made the same call for the same reason.
- **Apache-2.0** on `apps/` (RelayiumKit, the macOS app, and future iOS):
  GPL-family licenses' "no further restrictions" requirement is in tension
  with the App Store's terms — its DRM (FairPlay) and device-count limits
  are exactly the kind of extra restriction the AGPL forbids adding, which
  is why VLC was pulled from the App Store in 2011 under GPLv2. Apache-2.0
  carries no such clause, so the Apple clients can ship there without that
  conflict.
- **CC BY 4.0** on `docs/protocol/`: a wire protocol specification is
  meant to be implemented by as many people as possible. A code license
  would be the wrong tool here; CC BY 4.0 only asks for attribution and
  otherwise gets out of the way of third-party implementations — including
  competing ones.

## Prior versions remain MIT

Every version of this repository published before this relicensing was
released under the MIT License, and that grant is irrevocable. Anyone who
already has, or forks, a pre-relicensing copy of this code may continue to
use, modify, and redistribute *that copy* under MIT forever — this change
cannot and does not reach back and revoke it. Relicensing only governs
versions published from this point forward. If you need the last MIT
version, it is the last commit before this file changed, in this
repository's git history.

## Contributions

This project accepts contributions under the Developer Certificate of
Origin (DCO) — see [`DCO`](DCO) and [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md).
By signing off a commit (`git commit -s`) you certify you have the right
to submit it under the license that covers the directory it lands in.
